## 3.1 False Moving-Object Candidates

Previously identified issue:

The candidate pipeline could pair weak stationary PSF residuals with unrelated negative residuals separated by approximately 71–175 arcseconds, incorrectly presenting them as moving-object candidates.

A weak stationary residual must not become a moving-object detection merely because a negative residual exists nearby.

Required behavior:

Separate the scientific interpretation of:

- POSSIBLE_MOVING_OBJECT
- STATIONARY_SOURCE_RESIDUAL
- REGISTRATION_OR_PSF_ARTIFACT
- INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE
- SIGNIFICANT_FLUX_CHANGE

These classifications must have distinct scientific meanings.

Do not force every candidate into one category.

Moving-object classification must require evidence consistent with actual displacement across valid observations.

Inspect:
- Observation timing.
- Astrometric positions.
- Source morphology.
- Positive/negative residual relationships.
- Detection significance.
- Registration uncertainty.
- Data quality flags.
- Available independent observations.

A significant flux change is not automatically a moving object.

A difference-image dipole is not automatically a moving object.

A PSF residual is not automatically astrophysical variability.

If the available observations are insufficient, explicitly report insufficient evidence.

Do not introduce arbitrary universal thresholds.

If thresholds are needed:
- Derive them from available evidence or existing calibration.
- Document their scientific rationale.
- Expose provisional parameters explicitly.
- Make clear when they have not been calibrated.

## 3.2 Subpixel Candidate Coordinates

Previously identified issue:

Candidate RA/Dec could be calculated using integer pixel peak coordinates, introducing coordinate quantization.

Implement scientifically appropriate subpixel centroiding.

Requirements:
- Preserve integer peak coordinates.
- Calculate subpixel centroid coordinates independently.
- Preserve centroid uncertainty where available.
- Use the correct WCS associated with the relevant image.
- Do not pretend a subpixel centroid is more accurate than the image supports.
- Provide a documented fallback when centroid fitting fails.
- Keep peak and centroid coordinates separately available in the candidate model.

Candidate overlays and catalog cross-matches must use the correct coordinate representation.

## 3.3 Epoch-Specific WCS Cursor Coordinates

Previously identified issue:

The Explore view cursor could use epoch 1 WCS even when viewing epoch 2, a difference image, significance image, split view, or right-hand comparison pane.

Fix the coordinate transformation.

The cursor must use the WCS of the actual rendered image under the cursor.

Requirements:
- Epoch 1 uses epoch 1 WCS.
- Epoch 2 uses epoch 2 WCS.
- Difference products use their documented reference grid.
- Significance products use their documented reference grid.
- Split panes use the WCS corresponding to each pane.
- Sequence animation uses the currently displayed frame's correct WCS.

Do not use one global WCS for every visualization.

## 3.4 Candidate Overlay Positioning

Previously identified issue:

Candidate overlays used hardcoded canvas dimensions and inconsistent pixel-center conventions.

Fix the rendering transformation.

Requirements:
- Use actual rendered image dimensions.
- Support responsive resizing.
- Support mobile layouts.
- Preserve correct pixel-center conventions.
- Handle image scaling, cropping, and aspect-ratio changes.
- Keep overlays aligned when changing comparison modes.
- Avoid hardcoded 480px or other assumed canvas dimensions.

The overlay must use the same coordinate-to-screen transformation as the image renderer.

## 3.5 Footprint Geometry

Previously identified issue:

Footprint area calculations mixed pixel outer boundaries with pixel-center extrema.

Make the geometry consistent.

Clearly distinguish:
- Pixel-center coordinate extrema.
- Outer image footprint boundaries.
- Actual spherical footprint area.
- Bounding-box area.

Use the appropriate pixel boundary convention for each quantity.

Do not label a rectangular bounding-box area as the true sky footprint area.

Document the method and assumptions.

## 3.6 Deterministic WCS Tests

Previously identified issue:

A test selected the first FITS file alphabetically from a shared cache and accidentally loaded a larger parent image instead of the intended 64×64 fixture.

Fix the test fixture selection.

Requirements:
- Select fixtures by explicit identity or verified dimensions.
- Avoid filesystem-order assumptions.
- Keep the intended scientific assertion.
- Do not weaken tests to make them pass.
- Ensure tests remain deterministic regardless of cache contents.

## 3.7 WCS Documentation

The project uses a generalized SPHEREx-compatible TAN-SIP implementation.

Preserve its existing conventions unless independent evidence demonstrates a defect.

Update outdated descriptions that claim the system uses only rigid translation or PC-only WCS handling.

Do not remove generalized SIP support.

Document:
- Forward transformation.
- Inverse transformation.
- Pixel-coordinate convention.
- SIP coefficients.
- TAN projection.
- Reprojection behavior.
- Known limitations.

Do not claim external Astropy/WCSLIB validation unless it is actually executed.

## 3.8 PSF and Resampling Artifacts

Investigate the known behavior where bilinear reprojection can attenuate undersampled stellar peaks while preserving aperture flux approximately.

Do not simply increase peak brightness artificially.

Explain the difference between:
- Pixel peak preservation.
- Aperture flux conservation.
- Resampling-induced morphology changes.
- Registration artifacts.

Where necessary, expose the limitation in the scientific UI.

## 3.9 Science Integrity Requirements

Preserve:
- Difference = epoch2 - epoch1.
- Variance propagation based on the documented independent-error assumptions.
- Squared interpolation weights for variance resampling.
- DQ flag propagation.
- Real WCS transformations.
- Real FITS ingestion.
- Real metadata.
- Provenance tracking.

If the scientific assumptions differ in the current implementation, inspect and document the actual behavior rather than forcing this exact formula blindly.

The UI must never display a heuristic artifact score as a calibrated probability.

Never convert a significance value into a probability without a justified statistical model.

